Subject: [PATCH] x86/e820: insert the non-busy ranges without PCI as well

e820__reserve_resources() leaves persistent memory and soft-reserved
ranges out of iomem_resource, for drivers to claim; they are inserted by
e820__reserve_resources_late(), whose only caller (outside Hyper-V's
Gen2 special case) is pcibios_resource_survey(). With CONFIG_PCI=n that
never runs, so register_e820_pmem() finds no legacy PMEM resource and
an e820 type-12 range never becomes /dev/pmemN.

Call it from a subsys initcall when there's no PCI, which is the level
pci_subsys_init() runs at, before the device initcalls that need it.

--- a/arch/x86/kernel/e820.c	2026-10-30 13:06:41.000000000 +0100
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/e820.c	2026-11-04 12:17:16.579145162 +0100
@@ -1180,6 +1180,23 @@
 	}
 }
 
+#ifndef CONFIG_PCI
+/*
+ * Without PCI nothing calls pcibios_resource_survey(), which is where the
+ * non-busy e820 ranges (legacy and ACPI persistent memory, soft-reserved)
+ * get into iomem_resource. register_e820_pmem() then finds no
+ * PERSISTENT_MEMORY_LEGACY resource and no /dev/pmemN appears. Insert
+ * them at the initcall level pci_subsys_init() would have used, ahead of
+ * the device initcalls that look for them.
+ */
+static int __init e820__reserve_resources_nopci(void)
+{
+	e820__reserve_resources_late();
+	return 0;
+}
+subsys_initcall(e820__reserve_resources_nopci);
+#endif
+
 /*
  * Pass the firmware (bootloader) E820 map to the kernel and process it:
  */
